{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And when he blesses himself in his heart, saying, 'Peace shall be for me,' etc.</b> This whole passage speaks of one who sins in thought alone, as it is written: 'whose heart turns away,' etc., 'and when he blesses himself in his heart,' etc., 'for I walk in the stubbornness of my heart' — for idolatry, its essence is in thought, for there is no faith except in the heart. And therefore it is said concerning idolatry (Ezekiel 14:5): 'לְמַעַן תְּפֹשׂ אֶת בֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּלִבָּם' ('that I may take hold of the house of Israel in their hearts'). And in every mention of idolatry, nothing is recalled except the deed, as it is written (Deuteronomy 27:15): 'אָרוּר הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה פֶסֶל וּמַסֵּכָה' ('Cursed be the man who makes a graven or molten image'), etc. And from this there arise two kinds of error, for he will say: 'אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה' ('who makes') — and not from what is already made, that is, the gods of those nations that were mentioned above. The second error is that he will say: only a deed is forbidden, but not thought. This is what it says: 'וְהָיָה בְּשָׁמְעוֹ אֶת דִּבְרֵי הָאָלָה הַזֹּאת' ('And it shall be, when he hears the words of this oath') — that is, that oath which alludes to idolatry in particular, whose meaning is specifically about the deed; therefore he will find an opening to hang his error upon it — 'וְהִתְבָּרֵךְ בִּלְבָבוֹ לֵאמֹר שָׁלוֹם יִהְיֶה לִי' ('and he blesses himself in his heart, saying, Peace shall be for me') — that he pronounces a blessing of peace upon himself because the matter is in his heart. And this is what it says: 'כִּי בִּשְׁרִירוּת לִבִּי אֵלֵךְ לְמַעַן סְפוֹת הָרָוָה אֶת הַצְּמֵאָה' ('for I walk in the stubbornness of my heart, in order to add the watered to the thirsty'), for he will rely on this to escape punishment, saying: I sit among my people, and just as one who waters a thirsty field gives water along the way also to the watered field, so too, when HaShem sends blessing to the righteous who are thirsty for the word of HaShem — as it is written (Psalms 63:2): 'צָמְאָה לְךָ נַפְשִׁי' ('My soul thirsts for You'), etc. — along with them the wicked, who are sated from the service of HaShem, will receive blessing too, until they choose for themselves new foreign cults that have recently come near, for their souls are sated from the service of HaShem. He will also say: since the world is judged according to the majority, if so the merit of the righteous will stand for me as well, and He will forgive me for their sake." "Therefore it says: 'לֹא יֹאבֶה ה׳ סְלֹחַ לוֹ' ('HaShem will not be willing to forgive him'), for thoughts of the sin of idolatry are more severe than the transgression itself, because the essence of the matter is in the heart. For concerning one who serves idolatry it is written (Hosea 4:17): 'חֲבוּר עֲצַבִּים אֶפְרַיִם הַנַּח לוֹ' ('Ephraim is joined to idols — leave him alone') — that the Holy One, blessed be He, prolongs His anger even for idolatry; however, when they divided their heart from HaShem it is written (ibid. 10:2): 'חָלַק לִבָּם עַתָּה יֶאְשָׁמוּ' ('their heart is divided; now they shall bear guilt'). Therefore it says: 'כִּי אָז יֶעְשַׁן אַף ה׳' ('for then the anger of HaShem will smoke'), for the word 'אָז' ('then') refers to the present time — for then, immediately and at once, the anger of HaShem and His jealousy will smoke against that man — meaning against him alone, for since he sinned in secret, others are not caught in his transgression — 'וְרָבְצָה בוֹ כָּל הָאָלָה הַכְּתוּבָה בַּסֵּפֶר הַזֶּה' ('and all the oath written in this book shall lie upon him') — that is, the curses in parashat בְּחֻקֹּתַי. However, there all the curses are mentioned in plural language, so you might suppose that many will perish for his sin. Concerning this it says: 'וְהִבְדִּילוֹ ה׳ לְרָעָה מִכֹּל שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל כְּכֹל אָלוֹת הַבְּרִית הַכְּתוּבָה בְּסֵפֶר הַתּוֹרָה הַזֶּה' ('and HaShem will set him apart for evil from all the tribes of Israel, according to all the curses of the covenant written in this book of the Torah') — that is, the curses in parashat כִּי תָבוֹא, which were stated in singular language, to indicate that he alone shall die for his transgression, and all of Israel are clear." "And the fact that he called the Torah of the Priests simply 'סֵפֶר' ('book'), while the curses in parashat כִּי תָבוֹא he called 'סֵפֶר הַתּוֹרָה' ('book of the Torah'), is because all five books collectively are called 'תּוֹרָה' ('Torah'), and each book individually is called 'סֵפֶר' ('book'); therefore the book of the Torah of the Priests is called 'סֵפֶר,' for it does not comprise the entire Torah but is only one book of the set of five books. But this fifth book is called 'תּוֹרָה,' for it comprises all the Torah, for this reason it is called 'מִשְׁנֵה תּוֹרָה' ('repetition of Torah'), as it is written (Deuteronomy 1:5): 'הוֹאִיל מֹשֶׁה בֵּאֵר אֶת הַתּוֹרָה הַזֹּאת' ('Moses undertook to expound this Torah'). And this is what it says regarding the separation: 'וְהִבְדִּילוֹ ה׳ לְרָעָה' etc. 'כְּכֹל אָלוֹת הַבְּרִית הַכְּתוּבָה בְּסֵפֶר הַתּוֹרָה הַזֶּה' ('HaShem will set him apart for evil,' etc., 'according to all the curses of the covenant written in this book of the Torah') — for all of them were stated in singular language indicating this separation, to nullify from him the argument of 'לְמַעַן סְפוֹת הָרָוָה אֶת הַצְּמֵאָה' ('in order to add the watered to the thirsty'), so that he not say: I sit among my people. And for this reason he called the former 'אָלָה' ('oath,' singular) and the latter 'אָלוֹת' ('oaths,' plural), because the latter are doubled compared to the former — for in place of every single curse, two were found here, for the reason we explained in parashat כִּי תָבוֹא (Deuteronomy 28:15), either corresponding to the forty-nine gates of understanding, or corresponding to the Torah which is expounded in forty-nine facets — corresponding to each one, one blow — and here two, on account of the mutual responsibility. And what it says: 'וְרָבְצָה בּוֹ כָּל הָאָלָה' ('and all the oath shall lie upon him') — specifically that oath which is in the Torah of the Priests, for here it speaks of the sin of idolatry, and so too the oath in the Torah of the Priests speaks as well of the sin of idolatry, as it is written (Leviticus 26:30): 'וְהִשְׁמַדְתִּי אֶת בָּמֹתֵיכֶם וְהִכְרַתִּי אֶת חַמָּנֵיכֶם' ('and I will destroy your high places and cut down your sun-images'), etc. But those curses in the oaths of Moab do not mention the sin of idolatry. Therefore it says concerning the first oath: 'וְרָבְצָה בּוֹ' ('and it shall lie upon him') — for it alone shall lie beside him; however, regarding the separation alone — that HaShem will set him apart for evil from all the tribes of Israel — it says: 'כְּכֹל אָלוֹת הַבְּרִית' ('according to all the curses of the covenant'), etc., for these curses were stated in singular language. And what is said afterward: 'וְרָאוּ אֶת מַכּוֹת הָאָרֶץ' ('and they shall see the plagues of the land'), etc. — which implies that he is speaking of the punishment of the many — this refers to what was said above: 'אוֹ מִשְׁפָּחָה אוֹ שֵׁבֶט' ('or a family or a tribe'), for this speaks of the many who sin, and then they shall see the plague of the land, as will be explained shortly."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {2 "The Kli Yakar uses אָלָה (singular) and אָלוֹת (plural) to distinguish the curses in Leviticus (תורת כהנים) from those in Deuteronomy (כי תבוא); the Hebrew terms are retained where the singular/plural distinction drives the argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:04.497123038Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}